a seamless transition of power mint Oman  woke up to a new sultan while still in  mourning for their late monarch at the  royal palace haytham Ventana Khan Saeed  received world leaders there to offer  condolences and pay their respects to  omens new leader it's quite rare in the  Middle East for such a change in power  to be so smooth for the new leader to  inherit a country so stable and that is  a large part of the late Sultan cabooses  legacy at the Grand Mosque in omens  capital people shared memories often  speaking of Qaboos less as a ruler more  like family what they did for country he  brought from nothing like what you see  he used lost it's not a king pabu's  prioritize diplomacy over division  religious coexistence inside the country  political non-interference outside  neutrality as a way of keeping the peace  always very known in establishing peace  and we hope that his successor His  Majesty Sudan - aside to continue on the  same you know track and emphasize the  coexistence of cabooses reign was a time  of change spanning five decades but  there's been little pomp and  circumstance since his death the  transition to a new leader in a dynasty  that has ruled the country for fourteen  generations marked by the same quiet  dignity that caboose was known for  during his lifetime  Majid Abdullah Alice for worked for  Romans foreign ministry implementing  cabooses gentle approach to  international affairs he cared about  these people and he prioritized having  people's respect and he applied the same  principle when dealing with the rest of  the world  it's an approach ingrained in omens  institutions and unlikely to change  under new leadership Sultan Qaboos was  the quintessential elder statesman a  rare voice of reason who throughout his  public life held the middle ground in an  increasingly polarized Middle East and  Omani seemed confident their new Sultan  is the right choice to keep it that way  Zen Basra via al Jazeera Muscat
 

